---
import type { UniverseId } from '../config/taxonomy';
interface Props { id: UniverseId }
const { id } = Astro.props;
const sections = id === 'whiteout' ? ['Ground insulation', 'A complete sleep system', 'Shelter and a way back', 'Care and repairs', 'Hands, head and visibility'] : id === 'blackout'
  ? ['Before an outage', 'Choosing a power station', 'Wh versus W', 'Estimating runtime', 'Lighting and charging', 'Food refrigeration', 'Battery compatibility', 'Electrical safety']
  : id === 'fallout'
  ? ['Water planning', 'Food and rations', 'First aid organization', 'Sanitation', 'Shelter and warmth', 'Household checklist', 'Equipment limitations']
  : ['Evacuation bags', 'Carry weight', 'Navigation and communication', 'Weather protection', 'Respiratory limits', 'Fit and compatibility', 'Evacuation checklist'];
---
<section class="section guide" aria-labelledby="sector-guide" data-sector-guide>
  <p class="label">Preparedness planning · Updated <time datetime="2026-11-01">November 1, 2026</time></p>
  <h2 id="sector-guide">{id === 'whiteout' ? 'Plan warmth from the ground up' : id === 'blackout' ? 'Plan power around essential tasks' : id === 'fallout' ? 'Build a practical household emergency supply' : 'Prepare to leave with equipment you can carry'}</h2>
  <nav aria-label="In this preparedness guide"><ul class="guide-toc">{sections.map((title, i) => <li><a href={`#planning-${i + 1}`}>{title}</a></li>)}</ul></nav>
  {id === 'whiteout' && <>
    <section id="planning-1"><h3>Start with ground insulation</h3><p>A sleeping bag and a sleeping pad do different jobs. Your body compresses insulation beneath you; a pad adds resistance to heat flowing into the ground. Compare documented R-values for the exact model rather than thickness or cinematic artwork. The <a href="/gear/therm-a-rest-neoair-xtherm-nxt-review/">NeoAir XTherm NXT profile</a> explains the manufacturer’s 7.3 R-value and the unresolved retailer variant. An R-value is not a minimum safe air temperature.</p></section>
    <section id="planning-2"><h3>Build a complete winter sleep system</h3><p>Match pad width and length to your body and sleeping position. Keep your sleeping bag, spare layers and ground insulation dry. Check the bag’s documented temperature rating and the assumptions behind it; a pad does not replace clothing, shelter or a suitable bag. Practise assembling the full system near an easy exit before taking it into remote winter conditions.</p></section>
    <section id="planning-3"><h3>Plan shelter and a way back</h3><p>Check the forecast and local travel advisories before leaving. Agree on a route, turnaround criteria and an overdue contact plan. Shelter must address wind and precipitation as well as ground insulation. Carry accessible lighting and communication appropriate to your location; see the <a href="/blackout/#planning-5">lighting and charging guide</a> and <a href="/outbreak/#planning-3">navigation and communication plan</a>. A survival-themed product image does not establish a safe expedition limit.</p></section>
    <section id="planning-4"><h3>Inspect, repair and prepare a backup</h3><p>Inflate the pad and inspect the valve and seams before travel. Clear sharp debris from the sleeping area and follow the manufacturer’s care instructions. Carry a compatible repair kit and know how to use it. Plan what you will do if an inflatable pad leaks, wet gear cannot be dried or conditions exceed your equipment and experience. Avoid flames and unvented combustion inside shelters.</p></section>
    <section id="planning-5"><h3>Layer hands and head without losing visibility</h3><p>Compare mitts and gloves for fit, drying and dexterity. Add dry headwear and face layers with weather protection as needed. Snow-goggle tint must suit the actual light; no accessory makes a blizzard safe to travel through.</p><ul><li><a href="/gear/outdoor-research-alti-ii-gore-tex-mitts/">Outdoor Research Alti II GORE-TEX mitts</a></li><li><a href="/gear/hestra-army-leather-heli-ski-gloves/">Hestra Army Leather Heli Ski gloves</a></li><li><a href="/gear/smartwool-nts-250-balaclava/">Smartwool NTS AW15 250 balaclava</a></li><li><a href="/gear/smartwool-thermal-merino-reversible-cuffed-beanie/">Smartwool Thermal Merino Reversible Cuffed Beanie</a></li><li><a href="/gear/oakley-flight-deck-m-goggles/">Oakley Flight Deck XM / M snow goggles</a></li></ul></section>
  </>}
  {id === 'blackout' && <>
    <section id="planning-1"><h3>What to prepare before a power outage</h3><p>Walk through a day without electricity. Write down the tasks that cannot wait: contacting family, moving safely after dark and keeping essential supplies at the right temperature. Record each device, its power input and how long you need it. Charge portable batteries, locate cables and practise finding your lighting before the lights go out. Arrange a separate contingency with your care provider for powered medical equipment.</p></section>
    <section id="planning-2"><h3>How to choose a portable power station</h3><p>Choose output power for the appliances you need to run at the same time, including startup demand. Choose battery energy for the time between charging opportunities. Then check weight, Canadian voltage, allowed temperatures and the charging inputs in the exact model manual. Compare the documented <a href="/gear/ecoflow-delta-2-portable-power-station/">EcoFlow DELTA 2</a>, <a href="/gear/bluetti-elite-100-v2-solar-generator-kit/">BLUETTI Elite 100 V2 kit</a> and <a href="/gear/jackery-explorer-1000-v2/">Jackery Explorer 1000 v2</a> profiles by those requirements, rather than by headline output alone. A kit title does not prove the panel or cable included in a current offer.</p></section>
    <section id="planning-3"><h3>Battery capacity in Wh versus appliance power in W</h3><p>Watt-hours (Wh) measure stored energy; watts (W) measure the rate of energy use. A 1,000 Wh battery and a 1,000 W output rating describe different limits. An appliance may fit the inverter rating yet exhaust the battery quickly. Conversely, enough stored energy cannot make an undersized inverter start a compressor. Use the appliance manual and, where appropriate, a suitable energy meter to establish consumption.</p></section>
    <section id="planning-4"><h3>How to estimate runtime</h3><div class="notice" data-runtime-example><p><strong>Estimated runtime (hours) = Usable battery energy (Wh) / Average power consumption (W)</strong></p><p>Illustrative example, not a product test: assume a nominal 1,000 Wh battery, 80% usable energy after conversion losses and reserve, and an average 40 W load. That gives 800 Wh / 40 W = about 20 hours. The assumed 80% is not a measured efficiency or a promise for any listed station.</p></div><p>Usable energy changes with temperature, battery age, inverter losses, idle draw and output cutoff. Average consumption changes with operating mode and duty cycle; a refrigerator does not necessarily draw its nameplate power continuously. Include every connected load, keep a reserve and verify your own setup before relying on the estimate. Solar input varies with sun, shading and panel orientation.</p></section>
    <section id="planning-5"><h3>Emergency lighting and charging priorities</h3><p>Budget small loads first: a phone, a radio and task lighting may be more useful than a short period of electric heating. Choose hands-free light for movement and a lantern for a shared room. Compare the <a href="/gear/fenix-hm50r-v2-headlamp/">Fenix HM50R V2 headlamp</a>, <a href="/gear/fenix-cl26r-pro/">CL26R PRO lantern</a> and <a href="/gear/midland-er310/">Midland ER310 radio</a> for their own charging and battery requirements. Keep local emergency frequencies and written contacts available if mobile service fails.</p></section>
    <section id="planning-6"><h3 id="refrigeration-plan">Food refrigeration during an outage</h3><p>Health Canada advises keeping refrigerators at or below 4°C and freezers at or below −18°C. An unopened refrigerator generally keeps food cold for about four hours. Keep doors closed and use its <a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/food-drinking-water-safe-emergency.html">food and drinking-water emergency guidance</a> when deciding what to keep or discard; never taste food to decide whether it is safe.</p><p>A portable fridge adds a load to your battery plan. The <a href="/gear/bougerv-53-quart-portable-refrigerator-freezer/">BougeRV 53-quart fridge</a> and <a href="/gear/bougerv-rocky-v3-55qt-dual-zone-portable-car-cooler/">Rocky V3 dual-zone cooler</a> profiles explain model and package limits. The <a href="/gear/oria-wireless-refrigerator-freezer-thermometer/">ORIA two-sensor thermometer profile</a> explains its unconfirmed exact model and owner-reported features. A current reading or an alarm cannot establish a complete safe temperature history.</p></section>
    <section id="planning-7"><h3 id="battery-plan">Battery and charger compatibility</h3><p>Make a battery inventory by device: size, chemistry, voltage and charging method. The <a href="/gear/panasonic-eneloop-aa-rechargeable-battery-charger-kit/">eneloop AA kit</a> and <a href="/gear/panasonic-eneloop-aaa-rechargeable-battery-charger-kit/">eneloop AAA kit</a> use 1.2 V NiMH cells; matching physical size alone does not establish device compatibility. Their BQ-CC17 charger needs AC power. Check device instructions before substituting cells, never put non-rechargeable batteries in a charger, and compare cells alone if you already own a suitable charger.</p></section>
    <section id="planning-8"><h3>Essential electrical safety precautions</h3><p>Use the exact station and appliance instructions. Keep electrical equipment dry and ventilated, inspect cables and respect input limits. Connector shape alone does not establish solar-panel polarity, voltage or compatibility. Never backfeed a household outlet. Fuel-burning generators and cooking equipment must stay outside and away from openings; a portable battery is not a fuel generator. Follow <a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/services/policing/emergencies/preparedness/get-prepared/hazards-emergencies/power-outages/how-prepare.html">Government of Canada power-outage precautions</a> and local instructions.</p></section>
  </>}
  {id === 'fallout' && <>
    <section id="planning-1"><h3>Emergency water planning</h3><p>Separate drinking water from water for cooking and cleaning. Health Canada lists two litres per person per day and suggests two additional litres for cooking and cleaning. Adjust for pets, heat and individual needs. Store manageable quantities in clean, food-suitable containers and follow replacement instructions. The <a href="/gear/reliance-aqua-tainer-7-gallon/">Reliance Aqua-Tainer profile</a> covers container limits; a full large container is heavy and does not treat water. Follow <a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/food-drinking-water-safe-emergency.html">Health Canada emergency water guidance</a> and local water advisories.</p></section>
    <section id="planning-2"><h3>Food storage and ration selection</h3><p>Start with familiar shelf-stable food that your household can eat, including allergy and infant needs. Rotate dated supplies and pack a manual opener. Compare energy per actual package and the water, fuel and preparation each meal requires. A serving count is not a number of complete meals. The <a href="/gear/readywise-84-servings-emergency-food-bucket/">ReadyWise 84-serving bucket</a> needs preparation planning; the <a href="/gear/datrex-dx2400f-emergency-food-ration/">Datrex ration</a> and <a href="/gear/sos-3600-calorie-emergency-food-rations/">S.O.S. ration profile</a> discuss package and nutrition limits. No one ration establishes a balanced long-term diet.</p></section>
    <section id="planning-3"><h3>First aid organization</h3><p>Keep supplies visible, dry and easy to reach. Inventory what is actually inside the <a href="/gear/nanuk-904-first-aid-kit/">NANUK 904 first-aid kit</a> rather than assuming the case defines the contents. Add household medication lists, prescriptions and essential care instructions with professional guidance. Check expiry dates and arrange appropriate first-aid training; owning equipment does not establish treatment competence.</p></section>
    <section id="planning-4"><h3>Sanitation planning</h3><p>Plan hand cleaning, menstrual supplies, diapers if needed, waste bags and a safe place to keep waste away from food and stored water. The <a href="/gear/royale-velour-plush-thick-toilet-paper/">Royale tissue profile</a> distinguishes physical rolls from marketing equivalents. Paper alone does not replace water, hygiene supplies or a sewage plan. Follow local wastewater and public-health instructions when normal sanitation is interrupted.</p></section>
    <section id="planning-5"><h3>Temporary shelter and warmth</h3><p>Prepare layers, dry socks, blankets and a place to sleep that accounts for weather and household mobility. The <a href="/gear/sol-emergency-bivvy/">SOL emergency bivvy</a> is a compact emergency layer with condensation and durability limits, not a complete winter shelter. Keep a transport plan for reaching an official warming centre or shelter; do not use fuel-burning appliances indoors for warmth.</p></section>
    <section id="planning-6"><h3>Household emergency supply checklist</h3><ul><li>Stored water, familiar non-perishable food and a manual opener.</li><li>Lighting, radio, compatible spares and phone charging.</li><li>First aid, medications and personal-care supplies.</li><li>Warm layers, bedding, pet and infant supplies.</li><li>Copies of documents, cash, keys and written contacts.</li><li>A household meeting place, accessible evacuation plan and a schedule to check supplies.</li></ul><p>Use the <a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/services/policing/emergencies/preparedness/get-prepared/emergency-kits/get-kit.html">Government of Canada emergency kit checklist</a> as a starting point for at least 72 hours, adapting it to local advice and your household.</p></section>
    <section id="planning-7"><h3>Practical equipment limitations</h3><p>This sector’s name is a theme, not a claim of nuclear protection. The <a href="/gear/sawyer-squeeze-sp129-water-filter/">Sawyer Squeeze profile</a> describes its documented filtration scope; do not assume household filters remove radioactive contamination, dissolved chemicals or every pathogen. <a href="https://www.cdc.gov/water-emergency/about/">CDC water-emergency guidance</a> explains why boiling does not make chemically or radiologically contaminated water safe. A <a href="/gear/fnirsi-gc-01-geiger-counter/">handheld radiation meter</a> cannot certify that water, food or a location is safe. Use an authority-approved water supply and follow emergency officials rather than entering an area to take readings.</p></section>
  </>}
  {id === 'outbreak' && <>
    <section id="planning-1"><h3>Evacuation bag planning</h3><p>Plan the destination and route before the bag. Keep documents, medication information, water, food, light, communication and weather layers accessible. Adapt for children, pets and mobility needs. The <a href="/gear/511-tactical-rush72-2-backpack/">5.11 RUSH72 2.0 backpack</a> profile explains the carrying system; its volume does not prove that a filled bag is manageable. These are real preparedness notes. Post-apocalyptic scenarios are fiction and do not replace evacuation orders.</p></section>
    <section id="planning-2"><h3>Carry weight and equipment prioritization</h3><p>Weigh the loaded bag, including water, then try a short safe walk and adjust. Keep essentials accessible and remove duplicate tools before adding more. There is no universal safe pack-weight percentage for every person. A small repair tool can be useful for a defined task, but a knife is not universal protection. Read the <a href="/gear/leatherman-wave-plus-multitool/">Wave+ tool limitations</a> and use secure storage, appropriate training and lawful carrying practices.</p></section>
    <section id="planning-3"><h3>Navigation and communication</h3><p>Save offline maps, carry a local paper map and agree on meeting points and an out-of-area contact. Practise the route and keep charging capacity for your phone. The <a href="/gear/garmin-gpsmap-67i-satellite-gps/">Garmin GPSMAP 67i profile</a> explains subscription, activation and sky-view requirements; satellite messaging and SOS do not guarantee delivery or rescue. Review the <a href="/blackout/#planning-5">lighting and communication power plan</a> before relying on rechargeable equipment.</p></section>
    <section id="planning-4"><h3>Weather protection</h3><p>Choose layers for the forecast, duration and your own activity level. Keep insulation and spare clothing dry; a rain shell alone supplies little warmth. Compare the <a href="/gear/marmot-precip-rain-jacket/">Marmot PreCip rain-shell profile</a> and <a href="/gear/merrell-moab-3-mid-waterproof-boots/">Merrell Moab 3 footwear profile</a> for fit and use limits. Break in footwear before needing to walk far and check seams, closures and wet-weather performance yourself.</p></section>
    <section id="planning-5"><h3>Respiratory protection limitations</h3><p><a href="https://www.cdc.gov/niosh/docs/2018-176/default.html">NIOSH air-purifying respirator guidance</a> explains why selection must match an assessed hazard. Particulate filters are not gas or vapour cartridges; air-purifying respirators supply no oxygen. They must not be used in unknown, oxygen-deficient or immediately dangerous atmospheres. The <a href="/gear/3m-aura-9205-n95-respirator/">3M Aura 9205+ profile</a> and <a href="/gear/3m-6800-full-facepiece-respirator/">3M 6800 facepiece and filter comparison</a> describe different equipment, not universal outbreak protection. Follow public-health and emergency instructions.</p></section>
    <section id="planning-6"><h3>Fit, compatibility and correct selection</h3><p>Tight-fitting respirators need the correct size, appropriate fit testing and a user seal check as instructed for each use; facial hair can interfere with the seal. <a href="https://www.cdc.gov/niosh/ppe/respirators/fit-testing.html">NIOSH fit-testing guidance</a> explains why a model name alone is insufficient. Compatible connectors do not establish hazard suitability. Check approved component combinations and whether eyewear affects the seal. Work gloves and goggles protect only within their documented scope, and outdoor clothing is not chemical-protective clothing.</p></section>
    <section id="planning-7"><h3>Practical evacuation checklist</h3><ul><li>Check official instructions, the destination and an alternative route.</li><li>Take identification, keys, cash, essential medication and written contacts.</li><li>Carry water, manageable food, a light and charged communication equipment.</li><li>Pack dry layers, footwear, hygiene and household-specific supplies.</li><li>Check loaded weight, straps and access to essentials.</li><li>Tell your agreed contact where you are going when safe to do so.</li></ul><p>Adapt the <a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/services/policing/emergencies/preparedness/get-prepared/emergency-kits/get-kit.html">Canadian emergency kit checklist</a> to your route and local authority guidance. Equipment does not make a hazardous route safe.</p></section>
  </>}
  <p class="guide-footer">Equipment links lead to documentary profiles with unresolved limits, not endorsements or proof of compatibility. Read <a href="/how-we-research/">how we research equipment</a> or <a href="/gear/">browse all equipment categories</a>.</p>
</section>
